kunzite: the real cause of boot #56's panic, and write-combined RedwoodGPU buffers by default again.
pmap_sync_page_attributes_phys() did flush_dcache((vm_offset_t) (pp << PAGE_SHIFT), ...) with pp a
32-bit ppnum_t: the shift ran in 32 bits, so a page above 4 GiB lost its top bits. #56's page
0x1_60de0000 (page number 0x58378 with 16 KiB pages) became 0x60de0000, and flush_dcache's
phystokv_range() panicked: "illegal PA 0x60de0000; phys base 0x90000000". It is reached whenever a
page's cache attributes change from write-back (pmap_set_cache_attributes -> pmap_sync_wimg), e.g.
for a write-combined IOBufferMemoryDescriptor. Apple's IO-coherent SoCs never flush here; this
kernel does. Now ptoa(pp), which widens before it shifts. There is no "lower DDR bank": 0998's
reading, and topaz's old note in RedwoodGPU, were this truncation. With it fixed, RedwoodGPU's
buffers are write-combined by default again (0997's intent); gpu_cached=1 keeps them cached.

diff --git a/iokit/Kernel/arm/RedwoodGPU.cpp b/iokit/Kernel/arm/RedwoodGPU.cpp
index dd2d1b6..e25fab5 100644
--- a/iokit/Kernel/arm/RedwoodGPU.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/arm/RedwoodGPU.cpp
@@ -14,10 +14,10 @@
  * What there is of the interface is what freedreno uses to draw: parameters, buffers (made,
  * told their address in the GPU's address space, mapped, given back), submission of command
  * buffers, and waiting for one to be done. A buffer is wired kernel memory entered into the 3D
- * core's SMMU context; it is zero when made, and ordinary cached memory, pushed out of the cache
- * whenever the GPU is given something to do (see submit()); boot-arg gpu_cached=0 makes it
- * write-combined, as Linux maps them for freedreno, where the kernel can change its pages' cache
- * attributes (see RedwoodGPUStart).
+ * core's SMMU context; it is zero when made, and write-combined: what the program writes goes to
+ * memory, where the GPU reads it, and not into this processor's cache (as Linux maps them for
+ * freedreno; boot-arg gpu_cached=1 makes them ordinary memory, pushed out of the cache whenever
+ * the GPU is given something to do: see submit()).
  *
  * One request is not Linux's: a buffer that is the memory of an IOSurface (surfaceBuffer()), for
  * drawing into what the display shows and for reading pictures where other programs have drawn
@@ -325,16 +325,16 @@ RedwoodGPUStart(void)
 	uint32_t cached = 0;
 
 	/*
-	 * Translated: buffers are ordinary cached memory, pushed out of the cache whenever the GPU is
-	 * given something to do (submit()); gpu_cached=0 asks for write-combined. Not by default: a
-	 * write-combined IOBMD has its pages' cache attributes changed (mach_make_memory_entry ->
-	 * pmap_set_cache_attributes), which goes through the kernel's physical aperture, and that
-	 * covers 0x90000000 + 6 GB only, while the allocator also hands out pages below it, of the
-	 * lower DDR bank: kunzite boot #56 panicked at its first buffer, "phystokv_range: illegal PA
-	 * 0x60de0000; phys base 0x90000000" (topaz's old note here: "from the lower DDR bank the kernel
-	 * cannot map back"). Identity: pool memory is write-combined already.
+	 * Buffers are write-combined by default, as Linux's msm gives freedreno: nothing of the
+	 * program's to push out of the cache at each submission, and what the GPU writes is what the
+	 * CPU reads. gpu_cached=1 makes them ordinary cached memory. Making a buffer write-combined has
+	 * each of its pages flushed by physical address (pmap_sync_wimg ->
+	 * pmap_sync_page_attributes_phys), and that shifted the page number in 32 bits: kunzite boot
+	 * #56 panicked at its first buffer on page 0x1_60de0000 taken for 0x60de0000 ("phystokv_range:
+	 * illegal PA"). Fixed in pmap.c by the same patch; topaz's old note here ("from the lower DDR
+	 * bank the kernel cannot map back") was most likely that panic too.
 	 */
-	cached = redwood_gpu_identity() ? 0 : 1;
+	cached = 0;
 	(void)PE_parse_boot_argn("gpu_cached", &cached, sizeof(cached));
 	gGPUCached = cached != 0;
 	gGPULock = IOLockAlloc();
diff --git a/osfmk/arm/pmap/pmap.c b/osfmk/arm/pmap/pmap.c
index c240258..75366a1 100644
--- a/osfmk/arm/pmap/pmap.c
+++ b/osfmk/arm/pmap/pmap.c
@@ -6881,7 +6881,12 @@ void
 pmap_sync_page_attributes_phys(
 	ppnum_t pp)
 {
-	flush_dcache((vm_offset_t) (pp << PAGE_SHIFT), PAGE_SIZE, TRUE);
+	/*
+	 * SWEET (kunzite boot #56): ptoa(), not pp << PAGE_SHIFT, which shifts the 32-bit page number in
+	 * 32 bits: a page above 4 GiB (0x1_60de0000) became 0x60de0000, and flush_dcache's
+	 * phystokv_range() panicked on it. Apple's coherent SoCs never flush here; this kernel does.
+	 */
+	flush_dcache((vm_offset_t) ptoa(pp), PAGE_SIZE, TRUE);
 }
 
 #if CONFIG_COREDUMP
